 | `Pods` beside a `Podfile` | `pod install`  | <Level of=\"rebuild\" /> |"],[317,"| What | Location                                               | Level     |\n| ---- | ------------------------------------------------------ | --------- |\n| Logs | each item in `~/Library/Logs`, including crash reports | <Level of=\"safe\" /> |"],[144,"A sheet shows progress, then a summary of what was freed and anything that could not be removed, with the reason:"],[29,"About the sizes"],[183,"Add Reclaim"],[57,"After a scan, open **Duplicates** and click **Find Duplicates**. Reclaim compares files of 1 MB or more and lists only files whose contents are identical, byte for byte."],[252,"After updating Reclaim"],[180,"Allow it"],[73,"Answers about Reclaim for Mac, covering why there is no confirmation, protected folders, sizes compared with Finder, Full Disk Access, the root helper and privacy."],[64,"Anything another suggestion already covers."],[331,"Anything inside an app, framework, photo or music library, or other bundle."],[61,"Anything inside apps, libraries and other bundles."],[63,"App and system data in `~/Library`, `/Library` and `/Users/Shared`."],[276,"App caches and logs"],[278,"App leftovers"],[111,"App Store apps run in a sandbox that cannot read or delete files across the disk, which is the whole point of Reclaim."],[223,"Appearance"],[5,"Auto-clean"],[222,"Automation"],[93,"Because cleaning a developer Mac means deleting hundreds of items, and a confirmation for each one gets clicked through without reading. Instead, Reclaim explains every item before you tick it, ticks only Safe items for you, and refuses to delete protected folders at all. If you want a safety net, turn on Move to Trash."],[273,"Build caches"],[191,"Build from source"],[77,"Can I undo a deletion?"],[175,"Check the download (optional)"],[15,"Choose **Every day** or **Every week** in Automation › Scan automatically. A scan runs when one is due, including right after your Mac wakes, and by default only sends a notification such as *“12.4 GB can be reclaimed, 3.1 GB of it Safe.”*"],[48,"Choosing which copy to keep"],[208,"Click **+**, choose **Reclaim** in Applications, and make sure its switch is on."],[270,"Click **Remove Helper…** in System and enter your password. Everything else in Reclaim keeps working without it."],[123,"Click **Scan** on the Overview, or press ⌘ R. Reclaim reads every folder it is allowed to and shows a live count of files and bytes. A full Mac takes about a minute; click **Stop** at any time."],[261,"Click **System** in the sidebar, then **Install Helper…**."],[246,"Delete root-owned system caches, logs, sudo leftovers and local Time Machine snapshots on Mac with Reclaim's optional, locked-down helper."],[120,"Deleting"],[274,"Dependencies"],[159,"Developer Macs fill up quietly: `.next` and DerivedData folders, `node_modules` in every old project, Gradle and npm caches, emulator images, Docker build cache. Reclaim scans the whole disk, tells you what each of those is and what happens if you delete it, and removes what you tick in one click."],[42,"Disk Map"],[40,"Disk Map and duplicates"],[281,"Docker"],[24,"Docker and OrbStack"],[38,"Docker items share the Delete bar and the history with everything else. If Docker refuses an item, the summary shows Docker's reason and the item stays in the list."],[92,"Does Reclaim send anything over the network?"],[202,"Double-click `Reclaim.dmg` (or Reclaim in Applications). macOS says it cannot verify it. Click **Done**, not Move to Trash."],[198,"Double-click `Reclaim.dmg` and drag **Reclaim** onto the **Applications** folder in the window that opens. Then eject the disk image in Finder."],[195,"Download Reclaim.dmg, the latest version, ready to run: there is nothing to build. The release page also has `Reclaim.dmg.sha256` to check it, and the same app as `Reclaim.zip`."],[212,"Download the new release and replace the old app in Applications. Because each build is signed separately, macOS may ask you to repeat **Open Anyway**, you may need to switch Full Disk Access off and on again, and the helper, if you use it, asks to be paired again."],[171,"Download the Reclaim disk image for Mac, open it through Privacy & Security the first time, and give it Full Disk Access. Plus updating, uninstalling and building from source."],[303,"Downloads shared by every project, kept so they need not be fetched again. Deleting them only costs a re-download the next time a project needs them."],[44,"Duplicates"],[250,"Enter your password"],[272,"Every folder Reclaim suggests deleting on a Mac, including .next, node_modules, vendor, DerivedData, Gradle, npm and pip caches, Docker and system junk."],[219,"Every Reclaim setting explained, from Trash mode and scan scope to thresholds, excluded folders, the menu bar, schedules, auto-clean, low-space alerts and accent colour."],[149,"Every run is recorded: when, what was deleted or moved to the Trash, its size and anything that failed. The log is kept at `~/Library/Application Support/Reclaim/history.jsonl`."],[284,"Every suggestion comes from one of these rules. A rule looks only at a folder's name, where it is and the files beside it; once a folder matches, nothing inside it is listed again. `~` means your home folder."],[103,"Every user's home folder, and every mounted volume in `/Volumes`."],[101,"Everything in `/System`, `/usr` (except `/usr/local`), `/bin` and `/sbin`."],[72,"FAQ"],[326,"Files of 500 MB or more (adjustable) that no rule above covers, outside apps and photo libraries. <Level of=\"personal\" />"],[56,"Files of 500 MB or more that no other suggestion covers. Files inside apps and photo libraries are left out, because deleting part of a bundle breaks it. Everything here is <Level of=\"personal\" />: only you know whether you still need it. Change the size in Scanning › Large files from."],[107,"Files that were APFS copies of each other shared their storage; local Time Machine snapshots still hold the deleted data until macOS removes them; and macOS counts \"purgeable\" space as free. The figure settles within a few minutes."],[70,"Filter first to apply Keep to only some groups. Right-click a copy for **Keep Only This Copy**."],[323,"Folders in `~/Library/Containers` and `~/Library/Application Support` named after an app's identifier (such as `com.docker.docker`) when no installed app has that identifier. Apple's own folders are never included, and an extension's folder counts as its app's. <Level of=\"personal\" />"],[108,"Folders Reclaim cannot read are missing from the total (give it Full Disk Access), and the disk also holds snapshots and file-system data that are not files."],[332,"Folders you list under Never scan these folders."],[25,"Free disk space used by Docker Desktop, OrbStack and Colima. Reclaim removes build cache, unused images, stopped containers and volumes with Docker's own commands."],[327,"From Docker's own disk-usage report, when an engine is running. See Docker and OrbStack."],[220,"General"],[174,"Get the app"],[181,"Give it Full Disk Access"],[207,"Go to **System Settings › Privacy & Security › Full Disk Access**."],[203,"Go to **System Settings › Privacy & Security** and scroll to the Security section."],[47,"Hard links and APFS copies"],[62,"Hidden folders such as `~/.vscode`, and installed packages such as `node_modules` and `site-packages`: copies there belong to tools, and deleting one can break them."],[14,"Hide it with Show Reclaim in the menu bar. Scheduled scans and alerts keep running while Reclaim is open, even with its window closed."],[121,"History"],[45,"How duplicates are found"],[28,"How removal works"],[115,"How to scan your Mac with Reclaim, read the Overview, review suggestions by safety level, and delete caches, node_modules and other junk in one click."],[318,"If the app a cache belongs to is running, the item shows a warning. Quit the app first."],[213,"If you installed it: open **System** in the sidebar and click **Remove Helper…**."],[199,"If you used the disk image: Reclaim is not notarized by Apple, because notarization needs a paid developer membership, so macOS blocks it the first time with *\"Apple could not verify… is free of malware\"*. You do this once per version."],[36,"Images, containers and volumes that are in use are never listed. Select a row to read what it is and what happens after removing it."],[196,"In Terminal, in the folder you downloaded to:"],[104,"In your home: Desktop, Documents, Downloads, Movies, Music, Pictures, Public, Applications, the Trash, Library, and Library's main folders such as Application Support, Caches, Containers and Preferences."],[170,"Install"],[172,"Install from Terminal"],[248,"Installing"],[155,"Introduction"],[80,"Is a helper that runs as root safe?"],[78,"Is it safe to delete Safe items while I am working?"],[98,"It deletes only inside system cache and log folders and your own home folder, never a protected folder, and refuses tricks with `..` and symlinks. It only answers the exact copy of Reclaim that installed it. The source is public, and you can remove it any time from System."],[193,"It downloads the latest `Reclaim.dmg`, checks it against its published checksum, copies Reclaim into Applications and opens it. Run it again any time to update. You can read the script first."],[20,"It follows your Trash setting, is recorded in the history, and a notification tells you what was freed."],[197,"It should print `Reclaim.dmg: OK`."],[260,"Items that need the helper show a lock until it is installed, and Safe ones are only ticked for you once it is."],[122,"Keyboard shortcuts"],[280,"Large files"],[43,"Large Files"],[148,"Lists and the Disk Map update straight away; no rescan is needed."],[253,"Local Time Machine snapshots"],[6,"Low-space alerts"],[169,"macOS 14 Sonoma or later, on Apple silicon or Intel."],[262,"macOS asks in its own dialog. Reclaim never sees it."],[110,"macOS hides other apps' data, Mail, Messages and parts of your Library from apps by default. Without access, those folders are not measured or suggested. Reclaim works without it, but sees less."],[1,"Menu bar and schedules"],[277,"Mobile tooling"],[328,"Most of these belong to root, so deleting them needs the helper. Items macOS protects are skipped even then."],[176,"Move it to Applications"],[283,"Never suggested or deleted"],[204,"Next to the message about Reclaim, click **Open Anyway** and confirm with your password or Touch ID. If you allowed the disk image, macOS asks once more for the app itself the first time you open it from Applications: repeat these steps."],[210,"No app can grant this to itself, and Reclaim never asks again once it is on."],[113,"No. It has no account, no analytics and no update check. The scan and your file list stay on your Mac. Its only files are its settings and the deletion history."],[95,"Not when deleting permanently, which is the default. With Move to Trash on, items go to the Trash and you can put them back from there until you empty it."],[66,"Nothing is ticked for you. The **Keep** menu in the toolbar ticks all but one copy in every group shown:"],[8,"Notifications"],[16,"Off by default, and the only time Reclaim deletes something nobody ticked at that moment. When **Delete Safe items untouched for a day** is on, each scheduled scan deletes:"],[17,"only <Level of=\"safe\" /> items, never Rebuild or Personal ones;"],[94,"Only if you turn on auto-clean for scheduled scans, which is off by default. Even then it deletes only Safe items that nothing changed for 24 hours. See how auto-clean works."],[19,"only items where nothing inside changed for 24 hours, so a project you are working in keeps its caches."],[18,"only items you can delete without the helper;"],[7,"Open at login"],[177,"Open it the first time"],[179,"Open Privacy & Security"],[224,"Open Settings with **Reclaim › Settings…** or ⌘ ,. Changes apply at once unless noted."],[249,"Open System"],[182,"Open the setting"],[201,"Or allow it in System Settings:"],[173,"Or download the disk image"],[285,"Output your project's tooling recreates on the next build or dev-server start. That first build is slower."],[275,"Package caches"],[297,"Packages your project installed. Run the install command again before using the project."],[86,"Permissions"],[91,"Privacy"],[81,"Protected folders"],[334,"Protected folders, which cannot be deleted as a whole from anywhere in the app. See the FAQ."],[209,"Quit and reopen Reclaim, then scan."],[214,"Quit Reclaim and move it from Applications to the Trash."],[21,"Reclaim checks free space every few minutes and notifies you once when it drops below your threshold, 10 GB by default, and again only after it has recovered and dropped again."],[109,"Reclaim does not have Full Disk Access yet. Without it, macOS shows a permission dialog for each of those folders and holds the scan until you answer, so Reclaim skips them instead. Turn on Full Disk Access and scan again."],[156,"Reclaim is a free, open-source Mac app that finds build caches, dependencies, app caches and Docker leftovers, explains what is safe to delete, and frees the space in one click."],[106,"Reclaim shows the space files actually take on disk; Finder often shows how long files are. They differ for sparse files such as virtual-machine disks, which can claim 64 GB while using a few, and for small files, which still occupy at least one disk block. Hard-linked files are counted once."],[215,"Remove it from **Full Disk Access** and, if you turned it on, from **Login Items** in System Settings."],[189,"Remove its settings and history (optional)"],[188,"Remove the app"],[187,"Remove the helper"],[254,"Removing the helper"],[158,"Requirements"],[118,"Reviewing suggestions"],[74,"Safety"],[119,"Safety levels"],[184,"Scan again"],[114,"Scan and clean"],[116,"Scanning"],[221,"Scanning"],[4,"Scheduled scans"],[233,"Scope, the downloads age and excluded folders take effect at the next scan."],[41,"See where your Mac's disk space went with a size-sorted Disk Map, find large files, and remove duplicate files with APFS clones handled correctly."],[105,"Separately, macOS itself stops any app from changing its system files."],[218,"Settings"],[82,"Sizes"],[39,"Sizes come from `docker system df`. For an image, Reclaim shows the space only that image uses, which is what removing it frees; layers shared with other images stay. Totals can differ slightly from Docker's own summary because Docker rounds each size it reports."],[255,"Some caches, logs and leftovers belong to root, the system's administrator account. Reclaim can't delete them on its own; its optional helper can."],[245,"System and the helper"],[282,"System junk"],[30,"The `docker` command and a running engine: **OrbStack**, **Docker Desktop**, **Colima** or another. Reclaim finds the command in the usual places, even when it is opened from Finder. Open **Containers** in the sidebar; if the engine is not running, start it and click **Refresh**."],[112,"The app is not notarized, which requires a paid Apple developer membership. Follow Open it the first time, or build it from source."],[100,"The disk root, and every folder directly in it, such as `/Applications`, `/Library`, `/Users` and `/opt`."],[217,"The engine's tests run with `swift test` in `Packages/ReclaimCore`. They only ever create and delete files in temporary folders."],[23,"The first time you turn on a schedule or low-space alerts, macOS asks whether Reclaim may send notifications. You can change this later in **System Settings › Notifications**."],[102,"The folders directly in `/private`, and system folders such as `/Library/Caches` and `/opt/homebrew`."],[263,"The helper is copied to `/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools` and started by macOS whenever Reclaim needs it. After that, deleting works as usual: anything Reclaim may not delete is passed to the helper automatically when deleting permanently. In Move to Trash mode, such items are left alone."],[3,"The menu bar"],[117,"The Overview"],[268,"The pairing names one exact build, so after an update the helper refuses the new version. **System** then shows **Pair Again…**, which asks for your password once more. A paid Apple developer certificate would remove this step."],[200,"The quickest fix is one Terminal command, run before you open the disk image, that removes the quarantine mark macOS put on the download:"],[192,"The quickest way, and macOS opens Reclaim straight away with no warning. Paste this into Terminal:"],[132,"The sidebar has one entry per kind of suggestion, with its total size. What it finds lists every rule. Each list shows a checkbox, the item's name and folder, its size, its safety level and when anything inside it last changed."],[97,"The summary after deleting gives the reason for each item. Most often the files belong to root, for example ones created with `sudo npm install`. Install the helper to delete those. Files macOS itself protects cannot be deleted by any app."],[50,"The whole scan as folders and files, largest first. Each row has a bar for its share of the folder you are in, its size, the number of files inside and when anything in it last changed."],[99,"These are never deleted as a whole, however the path is written: with different capital letters, through a symlink, or as `/System/Volumes/Data/…`. Everything inside them, apart from the system folders, can still be deleted."],[190,"Tidy up permissions"],[269,"Time Machine keeps snapshots on your Mac's disk between backups, and they hold on to the space of files you deleted since. System lists them; with the helper installed you can tick and delete them. Backups on your backup disk are not affected, and macOS also removes local snapshots on its own when space runs low."],[141,"To stop Reclaim ticking Safe items after each scan, turn off Tick Safe items after each scan."],[279,"Trash and old downloads"],[178,"Try to open it"],[22,"Turn on **Open at login** in Automation so the menu bar and schedules are always running. If macOS asks you to approve it, Reclaim shows a button to **Login Items** in System Settings."],[186,"Uninstalling"],[185,"Updating"],[96,"Usually. Quit an app before deleting its cache, and stop a dev server before deleting its project's build cache. Otherwise the app may recreate files mid-way or show errors until restarted."],[2,"Watch free space from the Mac menu bar, get low-space alerts, and run daily or weekly scans that can auto-clean Safe caches untouched for a day."],[27,"What is listed"],[271,"What it finds"],[46,"What it leaves out"],[251,"What keeps it safe"],[157,"What makes it different"],[247,"What the helper handles"],[26,"What you need"],[142,"Whenever something is ticked, a bar at the bottom shows how many items and how much space, such as **Delete 23 items · 31.4 GB**. Ticks carry across every list, the Disk Map, Duplicates and Containers, and an item inside a ticked folder is never counted twice."],[87,"Why are my Desktop, Documents and Downloads missing from the scan?"],[83,"Why do Reclaim's sizes differ from Finder's?"],[88,"Why does it need Full Disk Access?"],[90,"Why does macOS block it the first time?"],[84,"Why doesn't free space go up by exactly what I deleted?"],[85,"Why doesn't the scan total match the used space?"],[75,"Why is there no \"Are you sure?\""],[89,"Why isn't it in the Mac App Store?"],[79,"Why was something not deleted?"],[76,"Will Reclaim ever delete things on its own?"],[206,"Without it, macOS asks for permission the first time any app opens Desktop, Documents, Downloads, iCloud Drive or another app's data, and the app has to wait for your answer. To keep scans from stalling, Reclaim skips those folders until it has Full Disk Access, and the Overview says so, with a button to the right settings page."],[216,"You need Xcode and XcodeGen."],[333,"Your Music, TV and Photos libraries, which macOS protects with permissions of their own."]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":15,"2":16,"3":17,"4":18,"5":